From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:06:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] build(package): separate dev and production channels, with
 our own CLSIDs

Patch 45 shipped by hijacking the Dev manifest, which meant the local
iteration build and the released build were one identity: installing a
release replaced the thing you develop against. Upstream already has the
machinery for this - four brandings selected by WindowsTerminalBranding,
each with its own manifest, alias and CLSID triple - so use it.

  Release branding  WintermGhostty      wtg.exe    the shipped package
  Dev branding      WintermGhosttyDev   wtgd.exe   local iteration

Both now carry freshly generated CLSIDs. That is the substantive half of
this change. COM registrations are machine-global, so inheriting
upstream's meant fighting the matching real Windows Terminal channel over
the "Open in Terminal" verb and default-terminal handoff - and moving the
shipped build to Release branding without regenerating would have moved
that fight from Windows Terminal Dev, a niche install, to Windows
Terminal stable, which is everywhere. Eight GUIDs in total: console
handoff, terminal handoff, shell extension and proxy stub, per channel.

The first three exist twice each - in the manifest and in a C++ header
that selects on WT_BRANDING_* - and both copies have to agree or the
class registers under one id and is created under another. The proxy stub
ids live only in the manifests; nothing in the IDL or C++ names them.

WtExeUtils.h follows: WtExe/WtdExe become wtg.exe/wtgd.exe, and
IsDevBuild() matches WintermGhosttyDev. It has to be the longer name -
"WintermGhostty" is a prefix of "WintermGhosttyDev", so testing for the
shorter one would call every production install a dev build.
